    {"problem": "No heat during a January cold snap and the house is a century semi", "solution": "Old brick with little insulation drops temperature fast, so there is no time to wait. The usual culprits are a cracked hot surface ignitor, a flame sensor coated in oxide, a stuck pressure switch or a failed inducer motor. We carry the common parts and take emergency calls evenings, weekends and holidays."},
    {"problem": "The furnace is wedged into a closet-sized room under the stairs", "solution": "Half the furnaces in Leslieville and Riverdale live in a space you cannot stand up in. We work in tight spots every week, and while we are in there we check combustion air, clearances and the vent run, because a cramped furnace room hides problems that cause repeat failures."},
    {"problem": "The furnace runs all night but the top floor never warms up", "solution": "That is rarely the furnace. Hundred-year-old ductwork, a single return in the hallway and closed dampers starve the upper floors. We measure static pressure and temperature rise, balance the dampers and tell you if a ductless head upstairs is the smarter fix than a bigger furnace."},
    {"problem": "Short cycling in a post-war Scarborough or Etobicoke bungalow", "solution": "Many of these bungalows got a furnace sized for a house twice the size. It heats the air near the thermostat, trips the high limit and shuts off. We confirm the cause with real readings, fix the limit, filter or airflow fault, and note the oversizing for when you replace."},
    {"problem": "Water pooling around a high-efficiency furnace in winter", "solution": "A condensing furnace makes litres of acidic water every day. In a cold Toronto basement the trap or drain line plugs or freezes, and the furnace shuts down on a pressure switch fault. We clear the trap, re-route the drain and check the side-wall vent for ice."},
    {"problem": "Old chimney, mid-efficiency furnace, and soot or a sharp smell", "solution": "An 80% furnace venting into an unlined brick chimney can back-draft, and that is a carbon monoxide risk. We inspect the heat exchanger, test CO at the registers and check the chimney liner. If the flue is failing, a sealed-combustion high-efficiency furnace vents out the wall and solves it for good."},
