  {"type": "split", "h2": "Why old Toronto ductwork is often the real furnace problem", "photo": "sys",
   "html": "<p>A surprising share of furnace repair calls in Toronto trace back to the ducts, not the furnace. Homes in the Annex, High Park and East York were ducted decades ago for gravity furnaces or a 60% unit, with big supply trunks and almost no return air. Put a modern blower on those ducts and the static pressure climbs. The high limit trips, the blower motor runs hot, and parts fail years early.</p><p>So before we replace the same blower motor twice, we measure. If the return side is starved, adding a return or enlarging the hallway grille is often cheaper than the part you keep paying for. If the trunk is leaking into the basement, sealing it brings the upstairs back to life and cuts your Enbridge bill at the same time.</p><ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Static pressure measured on every repair call</li><li>Return air fixed before parts are blamed</li><li>Temperature rise checked against the furnace rating</li><li>Duct advice written into the quote, never upsold on the spot</li></ul><p>For how furnace repair works everywhere we serve, see our <a href='/furnace/repair-maintenance/'>furnace repair and tune-up page for Ontario</a>. For everything else we do in the city, start at <a href='/toronto/'>HVAC in Toronto</a>.</p>"},
